    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter May 7 with a targeted offensive approach, leaning heavily into speed and favorable platoon matchups. **Pete Crow-Armstrong** headlines our board today with an elite 81.55 score; while his #8 spot in the order is suboptimal, the combination of a generous 4.77 xERA from the opposing starter and a significant 14% boost from platoon splits makes him our highest-ceiling asset. **Junior Caminero** also projects for a strong 71.24 performance, benefiting from a soft matchup against the lefty Bennett. Given our organizational priority on stolen bases, **Trea Turner** remains a mandatory start with a 66.01 score; while facing a mid-tier arm in Ginn, the high-efficiency environment created by a slow delivery to the plate elevates his stolen base probability, further justifying his position at the top of our lineup.\n\nThe remaining roster spots highlight the tension between raw power and situational efficiency. **Kyle Schwarber** (61.85) is locked into the lineup for his high xwOBA despite the platoon disadvantage against Ginn, while **Caleb Durbin** (42.10) serves as our most marginal start. **Caleb Durbin**\u2019s score is dampened by a difficult matchup against a 3.02 xERA pitcher and a bottom-of-the-order assignment, but his utility as a speed threat keeps him above our bench threshold. Looking at the depth chart, we are currently navigating a depleted bench; with **Yordan Alvarez**, **Hyeseong Kim**, and **Kazuma Okamoto** sidelined by scheduling, and **Christian Yelich** remaining on the IL, our optimization window is narrow. We are monitoring **Colt Keith**, **Zac Veen**, **Lars Nootbaar**, **Chase Meidroth**, **Gary Sanchez**, **Luke Raley**, **Josh Smith**, **Dillon Dingler**, **Carson Benge**, **Pedro Ramirez**, and **Luis Pena** closely for any late-breaking lineup shifts that might necessitate a pinch-hit maneuver if the game scripts turn unfavorable.",
